{
  "id": "COMPOUND:17751 - PROTEIN:1919",
  "verbalization": "Ganoderic Acid K is implicated in the pathogenesis of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASP) through a multi-step mechanism involving the targeting of Alpha globin and other molecules. The evidence suggests that Ganoderic Acid K acts as a modulator of the signaling pathways that lead to the dysfunction of WASP, which is central to the development of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. The direct interaction of Ganoderic Acid K with Alpha globin, followed by its targeting of (2S,3S)-2-(4-hydroxyphenyl)-3-methyl-2,3-dihydro-1-benzofuran-5-carbaldehyde, which in turn targets WASP, provides a clear, mechanistic link. This pathway highlights the potential of Ganoderic Acid K to influence cellular processes critical to the syndrome. The presence of multiple independent pathways converging on WASP underscores the complexity of the disease and the potential for multiple therapeutic targets. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Ganoderic Acid K may exert its effects through a series of molecular interactions that ultimately contribute to the pathophysiology of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.",
